    <!--
      The privacy screen. Every claim here is checkable against the code, and
      the crash-reporting item in M7 will make the first one false — at which
      point this text changes with it.
    -->
    <string name="privacy_title">Privacy</string>
    <string name="privacy_close">Close</string>
    <string name="privacy_intro">Starveil runs on this device. This page lists everything that leaves it, and there is nothing you did not ask it to send.</string>
    <string name="privacy_data_title">What is collected</string>
    <string name="privacy_data_body">One report exists, and only when something crashes. No account, no analytics, no advertising identifier. The project runs no server of its own — the report goes to Sentry, described next.</string>
    <string name="privacy_crash_title">Crash reports</string>
    <string name="privacy_crash_body">A crash sends the stack trace, app version, Android version and device model to Sentry. Node addresses, UUIDs, share links, subscription URLs and keys are replaced before it leaves the device. Nothing else is attached: no screenshots, no screen contents, and no record of which screens you opened. Settings → Diagnostics turns it off, and off means nothing is sent.</string>
    <string name="privacy_storage_title">What stays on the device</string>
    <string name="privacy_storage_body">Nodes, groups, routing rules, logs and latency history live in a SQLCipher-encrypted database in the app’s private storage. The database key is held in the Android keystore.</string>
    <string name="privacy_network_title">What it connects to</string>
    <string name="privacy_network_body">Two kinds of address only: the nodes and subscription URLs you imported, and — when the mainland preset is on — the rule-set files it downloads from jsDelivr or GitHub. Those requests reach a public CDN, not the author.</string>
    <string name="privacy_permissions_title">Permissions</string>
    <string name="privacy_permissions_body">VPN, to route traffic. Notifications, for the ongoing tunnel notice. The installed-app list is read only to draw the per-app routing screen, and it never leaves the device.</string>
    <string name="privacy_backup_title">Backups</string>
    <string name="privacy_backup_body">A backup is written wherever you choose, encrypted with scrypt and AES-256-GCM. The passphrase is never stored, and it cannot be recovered — without it the file is useless to everyone, including the author.</string>
    <string name="privacy_removal_title">Deleting it</string>
    <string name="privacy_removal_body">Uninstalling removes the database and the keystore entries with it. Backup files are yours to keep or delete.</string>
    <string name="privacy_contact_title">Questions</string>
    <string name="privacy_contact_body">Open an issue on the project’s GitHub repository.</string>

